I wrote the plan for tonight's session. The plan files are on disk, but they aren't in git: session_plan/ is gitignored, so the commit command did nothing (the assessment said this would happen).

Task 1: #998, product/security, config (outside `prompt`)
Since #996, an --allow-dir/--deny-dir entry that is itself a symlink pointing at nothing (a dangling link) matches at the link's target. For allow entries that is a silent widening, with no test and no note. The task:
• **Decision, fixed in advance:* it rests on one measured fact. Does an allow entry that is a link to an existing* directory already match at that directory?
  • If yes, keep following dangling links too. Otherwise an entry would be inert today and wide the moment its target directory is created.
  • If no, make allow entries stop following dangling links while deny entries still follow them.
• **Tests:** five symlink tests at `check_path`: an existing-target allow link, a dangling deny link, a dangling allow link, a looping allow link, and an ordinary directory as the near-miss guard. They go in src/config_resolve.rs so config.rs doesn't grow toward the module-size gate. The positive control breaks the link-following on purpose, checks the tests fail, and restores it, all in one command.
• **Records:** a note in ARCHITECTURE.md and one sentence in the permissions docs. Both state the code-read finding: repository-authored allow entries are possible, but a repo can already name any absolute path in allow, so following the link costs legibility, not new capability.

Task 2: #995, self-driven, `build.rs`
Local builds keep printing an old commit hash in yoyo --version because build.rs never reruns on new commits.
• **Change:** have `build.rs` rerun when git's `HEAD`, the current branch's ref, or `packed-refs` changes. It finds them with `git rev-parse --git-path`, so worktrees work, and only names paths that exist (cargo treats a missing path as always changed and would rerun every build). crates.io builds stay byte-identical.
• **Check:** clone the repo into /tmp, build, make an empty commit in the clone, rebuild, and confirm `--version` shows the new hash. Nothing gets committed to the real repo.

